# The start-headroom decision, run by BOTH implementations of the rule:
#
#   triggers::condition / triggers::standby_label  (Rust, fleet-view/)
#   cerebro--trigger / cerebro--standby-label      (emacs/cerebro.el) via emacs/cerebro-test.el
#
# WHY IT IS ONE FILE. It is `tests/lib/supervise.cases`' reason: one decision with two
# implementations in two languages, and a row they answer differently is a builder started for a
# bead that is gone, or a queue left unstaffed. Read that file's header for the conventions this
# one copies deliberately.
#
# THE RULE THE ROWS ENCODE, stated once so a new row can be checked against intent:
#
#   * HEADROOM is the work of a role that no in-flight session will take: available minus
#     in_flight, saturating at zero. `available' is `actionable_ids' for a planner and
#     `planned_ids' for an implementer; `in_flight' is the live sessions of that role holding no
#     bead. A row that NAMES a bead is not in flight - that bead has already left the available
#     set, so subtracting the row too would count it twice.
#   * Zero headroom -> the role's condition does not fire at all, whichever arm it would have
#     been, and the standby cell reads `→ 0 free'.
#   * Above zero -> the role's own condition decides as it always did, and the standby cell is
#     the role's own existing one. Headroom GATES the condition; it does not replace it.
#   * One number covers BOTH planner arms. A P0 is unplanned and not P4, so `p0_unplanned' is a
#     subset of `actionable_ids', and a planner in flight takes the P0 first because
#     `skills/plan-bead' plans every P0 before it looks at the buffer.
#
# THE COLUMNS, tab- or space-separated, in order:
#
#   role        `planner' or `implementer'.
#   available   `actionable_ids' for a planner, `planned_ids' for an implementer - the count.
#   in_flight   live sessions of that role holding no bead.
#   p0          `yes' when the first actionable bead is a P0, `-' for an implementer row.
#   planned     the buffer count, `-' for an implementer row.
#   want        `planner_want()', `-' for an implementer row.
#   arm         which arm fired: `p0' `buffer' `planned' or `none'. NOT the reason string - the
#               two views spell those differently and always have.
#   cell        `zero' when the standby cell is `→ 0 free', `role' when it is the role's own
#               existing cell.

# role         available in_flight p0   planned want arm      cell
planner        5         0         no   0       4    buffer   role
planner        5         2         no   0       4    buffer   role
planner        5         5         no   0       4    none     zero
# Saturating, not wrapping: more sessions in flight than candidates.
planner        1         3         no   0       4    none     zero
planner        1         1         no   0       4    none     zero
planner        1         1         yes  0       4    none     zero
planner        1         0         yes  0       4    p0       role
planner        1         0         yes  4       4    p0       role
planner        0         0         no   0       4    none     zero
# A full buffer with candidates left: headroom is three and the answer is still `none' - headroom
# gates the condition, it does not replace it - with the cell `role', because the row is waiting
# for the buffer and not for headroom.
planner        3         0         no   4       4    none     role
implementer    1         0         -    -       -    planned  role
implementer    1         1         -    -       -    none     zero
implementer    4         2         -    -       -    planned  role
implementer    4         9         -    -       -    none     zero
implementer    0         0         -    -       -    none     zero
